    "## 9. Results & Interpretation\n",
    "**Summary of Key Findings**\n",
    "Our analysis demonstrates that the Electric Vehicle landscape in Washington State is highly deterministic in some areas and complex in others. We successfully built a highly accurate classification model (98% accuracy) to automate CAFV eligibility checks based purely on vehicle make, model, and EV type. Conversely, predicting geographic administrative boundaries (Legislative Districts) proved more challenging (71% variance explained), heavily relying on high-level city and utility data. Furthermore, our clustering model revealed that the market is clearly segmented by generational leaps in technology, with `Electric Range` and `Model Year` creating natural dividing lines among consumers.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Business and Policy Insights**\n",
    "1. **Infrastructure Focus:** The overwhelming concentration of EVs in King County and Seattle suggests that while early adoption was urban-centric, state policymakers need to incentivize charging infrastructure in rural and eastern counties to balance the grid and promote statewide adoption.\n",
    "2. **Eligibility Automation:** The near-perfect accuracy of the CAFV classification model suggests that the Department of Licensing could entirely automate eligibility approvals using a simple rules-based or machine-learning system tied to the manufacturer's vehicle specifications.\n",
    "3. **Market Dominance:** Tesla's massive market share indicates that Washington's EV infrastructure (like charging plug standards) will be heavily influenced by Tesla's proprietary network for the foreseeable future.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
